[Event "London System for White"]
[Lesson "london-idea"]
[LessonTitle "The London idea"]
[Step "explain"]
[TeacherNotes "The idea in one line: build a simple, solid setup that you can use against many replies | Name the pieces as you go: pawn d4, knight f3, bishop f4 | You learn one plan well instead of many lines badly | People say the name comes from a chess event held in London long ago."]
[Mistakes "Thinking you can play the same moves with your eyes closed: always check what Black just did | Moving the same piece twice | Forgetting that Black also makes threats"]
[ClassQuestions "Which pieces have we moved after three moves? | Why is a simple setup helpful for a beginner? | Where does the bishop on f4 point?"]

{The idea: build a simple, solid setup with the same few moves every game. The star is the bishop on f4. [%cal Gd2d4,Gg1f3,Gc1f4]}
1. d4 {The pawn takes space in the center. [%cal Gd2d4]} 1... d5
2. Nf3 {The knight comes out and guards d4 and e5. [%cal Gg1f3]} 2... Nf6
3. Bf4 {This is the London System. The bishop is out, and it is outside the pawn chain. [%cal Gc1f4] [%csl Gf4]} *

[Event "London System for White"]
[Lesson "london-idea"]
[Step "find-move"]
[FEN "rnbqkb1r/ppp1pppp/5n2/3p4/3P4/5N2/PPP1PPPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 2 3"]
[Hint "Use the bishop that starts on c1."]
[Hint "Move the bishop from c1 to f4."]

{White to move. Bring out the London bishop.}
3. Bf4 {The bishop steps out to f4, where it has lots of room. [%cal Gc1f4]} *

[Event "London System for White"]
[Lesson "london-idea"]
[Step "line-drill"]
[Side "white"]
[Hint "Start with the queen's pawn."]
[Hint "Then the knight to f3."]
[Hint "Last, the bishop from c1 comes out."]

{Play the first three moves of the London System as White.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Bf4 *

[Event "London System for White"]
[Lesson "bishop-first"]
[LessonTitle "Why the bishop goes first"]
[Step "explain"]
[TeacherNotes "A pawn on e3 closes the diagonal for the c1 bishop | Bishop first, then the pawn: the bishop gets out before the door shuts | Walk a student up to the board and show the locked bishop with their arm | This idea works for the c8 bishop too"]
[Mistakes "Playing e3 on move two and trapping the bishop behind it | Playing the bishop out and then forgetting the e3 pawn | Leaving the bishop on f4 unprotected"]
[ClassQuestions "What does the pawn on e3 do to the bishop on c1? | Why do we play e3 after the bishop is out? | Which pawn will protect the bishop on f4 later?"]

{Look what happens if White plays e3 too soon. The pawn blocks the bishop on c1. The bishop can only step to d2. [%cal Re2e3,Rc1f4] [%csl Rc1]}
1. d4 d5 2. e3 {The pawn on e3 shuts the diagonal. [%csl Rc1,Re3]} 2... Nf6 *

[Event "London System for White"]
[Lesson "bishop-first"]
[Step "explain"]

{Now the right order. First the bishop goes out. Then the pawn goes to e3 and guards the bishop. [%cal Gc1f4,Ge2e3,Ge3f4]}
1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Bf4 {Bishop first.} 3... e6 4. e3 {Now the pawn shuts the door behind the bishop and guards it. [%cal Ge3f4] [%csl Gf4]} *

[Event "London System for White"]
[Lesson "bishop-first"]
[Step "find-move"]
[FEN "rnbqkb1r/pppp1ppp/4pn2/8/3P4/5N2/PPP1PPPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 0 3"]
[Hint "Use the bishop that starts on c1."]
[Hint "Move it to f4, before you play e3."]

{White to move. Get the bishop out before the e-pawn moves.}
3. Bf4 {Bishop first. The e-pawn can move next. [%cal Gc1f4]} *

[Event "London System for White"]
[Lesson "bishop-first"]
[Step "quiz"]
[Question "Which order is better for the London System?"]
[Choices "Pawn to e3, then bishop to f4 | Bishop to f4, then pawn to e3"]
[Answer "2"]
[Explanation "After e3 the c1 bishop cannot reach f4. So the bishop goes first."]

{Think about the bishop on c1.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Bf4 *

[Event "London System for White"]
[Lesson "bishop-early"]
[LessonTitle "The quick version: 2.Bf4"]
[Step "explain"]
[TeacherNotes "Some players put the bishop out on move two, before the knight | This is the same plan with a different move order | The opening has its own name in the index: Accelerated London System | The knight on f3 can still come next"]
[Mistakes "Forgetting that Black may reply differently, for example with a pawn to c5 | Not developing the knight after the bishop is out"]
[ClassQuestions "What is different about this move order? | Which piece should come out next? | Why can the bishop come out so early?"]

{You can also bring the bishop out right away. The pieces are the same, only the order changes. [%cal Gc1f4] [%csl Gf4]}
1. d4 d5 2. Bf4 {The bishop comes out on move two. [%cal Gc1f4]} 2... Nf6 3. Nf3 {The knight follows. Now it is the same setup as before. [%cal Gg1f3]} *

[Event "London System for White"]
[Lesson "bishop-early"]
[Step "find-move"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/ppp1pppp/8/3p4/3P4/8/PPP1PPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 2"]
[Hint "Use the bishop that starts on c1."]
[Hint "Move the bishop from c1 to f4."]

{White to move. Play the London bishop right now.}
2. Bf4 {The bishop is out on move two. [%cal Gc1f4]} *
